    ;; The compact unicode layout (queue 270; doc/python/PYTHON-SURVEY.md, "As built: 270"; 262's DECISION (a), the user
    ;; 2026-11-10, the VM's proposal Q3). Without Py_LIMITED_API, CPython 3.14.10's Include/cpython/unicodeobject.h reads a
    ;; str in place: PyUnicode_GET_LENGTH its length, PyUnicode_KIND, PyUnicode_IS_ASCII and PyUnicode_IS_COMPACT its state
    ;; bits, PyUnicode_DATA its characters (after the PyASCIIObject of a compact ASCII string, after the
    ;; PyCompactUnicodeObject of another compact one, at data.any of a legacy one: a str subclass's instance), and
    ;; PyUnicode_WRITE writes them. So a str's handle is a mirror as a tuple's is (capi_mirror.go): the layout CPython
    ;; would have made for its characters, in its canonical kind (1, 2 or 4 bytes a character, the ASCII flag). Strs are
    ;; immutable, so Go never writes a mirror again; the one exception is a str PyUnicode_New made, which C fills in place
    ;; (PyUnicode_WRITE, PyUnicode_Fill, PyUnicode_CopyCharacters, memcpy): its characters are copied back to the pyi str
    ;; when it is synchronized (syncFromC), until Python has it.
    ;;
    ;; Ported from CPython 3.14.10's Objects/unicodeobject.c (PyUnicode_New, PyUnicode_Fill, PyUnicode_CopyCharacters and
    ;; _copy_characters, PyUnicode_FromKindAndData, as_ucs4, unicode_kind_name, the PyUnicodeWriter API); the PSF's
    ;; license, LICENSE.md.


